UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 27, Lesson 2: Planning an explanation', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Planning an explanation means deciding the question (how/why), the stages in order and the key cause–effect links before writing.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan three stages for: Why does a shadow change length during the day?\n\nFEEDBACK\nStages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes.\n\nCHECK\nA plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan two explanations on different topics using question, materials/facts needed, ordered stages and one cause–effect link per stage. Do not write the full text yet.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Planning an explanation means deciding the question (how/why), the stages in order and the key cause–effect links before writing.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan two explanations on different topics using question, materials/facts needed, ordered stages and one cause–effect link per stage. Do not write the full text yet. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Stages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes. Reasoning: I used explanation by attending to plan and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation. Correction: Stages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes. Why: the correction restores accurate use of explanation.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating explanation on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with explanation, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Stages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes. This matches the lesson skill for explanation.","options":["Stages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes.","A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Plan three stages for: Why does a shadow change length during the day?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Stages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes.","Careful use of plan and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation.","Planning an explanation means deciding the question (how/why), the stages in order and the key cause–effect links before writing."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising explanation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named plan and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the plan and explain why it matters used."],"question":"A learner is practising explanation. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries explanation.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show explanation and say what they do."],"question":"Read: \"At dawn, Mina whispered to her goat as frost glittered on the fence.\" Which choice best uses explanation from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Stages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes.","A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about explanation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise plan and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new.","A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about explanation.","Retype the worked model as the final answer."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of explanation to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating explanation on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Stages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes. Reasoning: I used explanation by attending to plan and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with explanation, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation. Correction: Stages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes. Why: the correction restores accurate use of explanation.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w27-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise explanation.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use explanation with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify plan and explain why it matters.","I can practise stages independently on a new prompt.","I can notice how/why needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Planning an explanation means deciding the question (how/why), the stages in order and the key cause–effect links before writing. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Planning an explanation means deciding the question (how/why), the stages in order and the key cause–effect links before writing.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about explanation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nQuestion: How does a seed grow? Stages: water absorbed → seed swells → root emerges → shoot grows toward light.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan three stages for: Why does a shadow change length during the day?\n\nFEEDBACK\nStages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes.","explain":"Planning an explanation means deciding the question (how/why), the stages in order and the key cause–effect links before writing.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Explanation plan with ordered growth stages.","caption":"Plan stages before full sentences.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Question</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How does a seed grow?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stages</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">water; swell; root; shoot</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Question: How does a seed grow? Stages: water absorbed → seed swells → root emerges → shoot grows toward light. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Question: How does a seed grow? Stages: water absorbed → seed swells → root emerges → shoot grows toward light. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about explanation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nQuestion: How does a seed grow? Stages: water absorbed → seed swells → root emerges → shoot grows toward light. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Ordered shadow stages contrasted with a jumble.","caption":"Order keeps the how/why clear.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shadow plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">sun position → angle → length</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Random jump</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">stages out of order</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about explanation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation. Here is a more productive model: Question: How does a seed grow? Stages: water absorbed → seed swells → root emerges → shoot grows toward light.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStages might include sun position changes → light angle changes → shadow length changes.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. A plan that jumps randomly will produce a confusing explanation. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about explanation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPlanning an explanation means deciding the question (how/why), the stages in order and the key cause–effect links before writing. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 27, Lesson 2: Planning an explanation","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Procedures and explanations","week":27,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nQuestion: How does a seed grow? Stages: water absorbed → seed swells → root emerges → shoot grows toward light.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Explanation plan with ordered growth stages.","caption":"Plan stages before full sentences.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Question</text><text x=\"28\" y=